[PLAN] feat.mm.gc_poison_sweep: 
The handle rules (docs/technical/36_gc_handle_rules.md) checked against what the other plans
exercise: their tests are run again with safepoint poisoning (--gc-check poison), in which every
safepoint moves every young object and overwrites where it was, so a C++ pointer kept across a
safepoint, a missing root or a stale interior pointer ends the program with a report instead of
going unnoticed. The assertions of each test are kept: the program must print what it prints
without the mode.

The plans are named by directory, so an operator or a module added later is covered as soon as
it has a test there. Left out: the slow model tests of autodiff and onnx (run them with
`node scripts/test.js feat/modules/autodiff.plan.toml` after adding `--gc-check poison` when touching those
modules), tests that choose their own collector settings, benchmarks and differential tests.
A test that cannot run in the mode says `sweep = false` and why.

The fast sample is risk-based (`fast_sample`, `risk_tags`, `risk_sample`): a source plan whose
`tags` name collections, closures or iterations (one of `risk_tags`) keeps three sampled tests,
every other plan one; the sample prefers a test that runs its program to completion. Those
operators move objects across safepoints in more ways, so one sample is thin there, while a
plan that only parses or computes scalars needs one safepoint poisoned to catch a kept pointer
or a missing root. The default developer run therefore poisons a safepoint in every plan; the
full sweep of every test is `--tier milestone` (or `npm run test:all`: the unsampled copies are the
milestone layer, and `--tier all` adds the sampled fast ones).

The plans of the rewrite module and of the standard rule libraries keep three samples as well
(`rules`): a rule's condition decides which nodes a rewrite matches, and a defect in one of those
conditions is invisible in the copies a single sample leaves unrun.

