[PLAN] feat.mm.retype: 
A frozen graph's node types are written into the record when it is encoded (`GCNode::typeId`,
from the draft's node type or from the slot the node reads), and its frame layout, function type
and static-area layout are written beside it. Nothing of that is a column a pass can rewrite
afterwards, and every graph that calls it, every reader that holds the version and the back end
that laid out the frame were built for the type it has. Replacing a type is therefore a rebuild:
decode the graph, change the types -- nodes, slot layouts, signature, closure and the form ports
of the regions -- and commit it as a new version, leaving the old one for its readers
(camel/runtime/retype.h).

The two cases below are the primitive's acceptance. The first is the trivial substitution
(`float` -> `int`, everything else unchanged) on four back ends, together with the proof that the
rebuild adds nothing of its own: a rebuild under an empty substitution has to be recognized as
the same content, and the graph the program was built from has to be exactly what it was. The
second is the probe the defunctionalization step needs: a `(float, (float) => float)` pair -- a
value and its pullback -- replaced by an `enum` with one variant carrying the same tuple, changing
no node. What proves that swap is the commit's own validation: a JOIN and the arms that feed it, a
function's signature and the call that reads it all have to be one type afterwards.

