[PLAN] feat.mm.context_lifetime: 
The lifetime of a `Context` that is not the only one of the process.

A process may hold more than one context: a tool, a language server or `--timeit` builds a second
`Context::create` beside a running one. Each context owns the modules, the executor manager and
the builders it made, and those objects refer back to the context they belong to. When that
back-reference was an owning one, the context was kept alive by its own members: `held` read 5
(the pass's own reference, `Context::exeMgr_`, the built-in module, and the two executor factories
that module registers in `src/builtin/modules/globals.cpp`), the context never reached its
destructor, and a context that was not the last one of the process was never destroyed. Everything
its destructor does then happened only for the last context: giving back the object root set and
the graph sweeper it registered, and draining the deferred-release queue its teardown queued.

`test::context_lifetime` reads all of it. It builds a second context in a scope of its own, reads
how many owners it has while the scope holds one of them, ends the scope, and reads what the
teardown left. One line per pass, read once per back end:

  CONTEXTS-PROBE held=<n> live_before=<n> live_second=<n> live_after=<n> rootset_before=<n>
                 rootset_second=<n> rootset_after=<n> root_given_back=<0|1>
                 deferred_before=<0|1> deferred_after=<0|1> ran=<0|1> alive=<0|1>

  held              the second context's owner count while the scope holds one of them
  live_before       the contexts alive when the pass starts (liveContextCount)
  live_second       the contexts alive while the second one is being used
  live_after        the contexts alive after its scope ended
  rootset_before    how many context root sets the allocator holds before the second is built
  rootset_second    how many it holds while both are alive
  rootset_after     how many it holds after the scope: the running context's own entry is one
  root_given_back   the second context's own entry is no longer registered
  deferred_before   a release the pass queued inside the scope is queued
  deferred_after    it is still queued after the scope
  ran               and the queue was drained
  alive             the running program survived a major collection taken afterwards

The numbers this reads: `held=5`, `live_after` one above `live_before`, the second context's entry
still registered after the scope (`root_given_back=0`), and `deferred_after=1` with `ran=0` -- the
cycle, a second context never destroyed. With the back-references non-owning (a module and the
executor manager hold `Context::handle()`, which is a handle that does not own): `held=1`,
`live_after` back to `live_before`, `root_given_back=1`, and `deferred_after=0` with `ran=1`.

The allocator keeps one object root set and one graph sweeper per owner (`registerObjectRootSet`,
`registerGraphSweeper`): the running context's entry is there throughout, the second context's is
added beside it while it lives and given back by its destructor, and nothing is displaced. `alive`
is therefore about the running context's own entry surviving the major collection the pass takes
after the scope, not about an entry put back.

