[PLAN] feat.modules.stdlib_reduced: The reduced-precision floating types are scalar types of the language (design principles 7.3): `bf16` and `f16` have literals that round once to the nearest member of the type, written conversions (`bf16(x)`, `f16(x)`, `x as f32`) that need no numeric permission, the scalar arithmetic and comparison of any other float computed in f32 and rounded once into the element type, and a place in an array; `is_float` names both, so `is_numeric` and `is_ordered` inherit them; and `array::sum` over one of them accumulates in f64 and rounds once, as the tensor reduction does. What is left refused is refused by name, with the element type and the operation that cannot take it.
