[PLAN] feat.modules.rules_differential: The standard rule library (`stdlib/rules.cml`) checked by differential execution rather than by sampling the two sides: every exported rule is written as a witness program containing its left side at concrete types and constants, the rule is applied to that witness alone, and the witness is run before and after the rewrite on one finite domain of inputs on one interpreter. A rule that stops firing, a rule whose two readings differ by one bit (a `-0.0` where a `+0.0` was), a rule of the library that no witness covers, and a label that outlives its rule all fail the check. The three accuracy laws are declared out of scope with the reason that their proposition is an error bound, not equality. Coverage and the proposition of each rule are read from the library's own `#requires` lines by `verify/differential_equivalence.mjs`, so the list of rules cannot drift away from the check.
[PASS] rules.differential_every_law_keeps_its_value         506.72 ms

[PLAN] feat.modules.rules_proofs: The standard rule library (`stdlib/rules.cml`) decided by an SMT solver instead of sampled: each rule's two sides become a proposition, its numeric licence becomes a premise (bit identity for a rule that asks for none, equality over the reals for `reassociate`, the two further statements that make that reading legitimate for `no_infs` and `no_signed_zeros`, and an error bound for `refine_accuracy`, which is reported `unknown` rather than forced into an equality), and the solver answers `proved`, `refuted` with the counterexample model, or `unknown` with the reason. The licence names are read from `src/core/operator_semantics.cpp` and the guards from the library's own helper definitions, so neither list can drift away from the check. The solver is an optional dependency: the test skips with the checker's install instructions when no solver is on the host, and never reports that it proved something it never asked. Two safeguards run inside the same checker -- four ground queries whose answers are known, and a deliberately broken rule that must come back refuted with a readable model -- so a translation that stopped deciding anything fails before a `proved` does.
[PASS] rules.proofs_every_law_holds_under_its_licence         59.03 s

[PLAN] feat.compile.return_after: A `return` gives the value of the block it is written in (design principles 7.3), so a statement written after it in the same block is never reached: it is a compile-time error, `StatementAfterReturn`, at the first such statement. An arm's `return` keeps giving the arm's value, and a second `return` handing the same exit is refused instead of overwriting it -- under a declared `T!` it used to hand on a value of the wrong shape and crash the node VM.
